--- a/apps/docs/content/guides/self-hosting/updating.mdx
+++ b/apps/docs/content/guides/self-hosting/updating.mdx
@@ -0,0 +1,166 @@
+---
+title: 'Update your self-hosted deployment'
+description: 'Update an existing self-hosted Supabase deployment to a newer release with update.sh, preserving your configuration and data.'
+subtitle: 'Update an existing self-hosted Supabase deployment to a newer release with update.sh, preserving your configuration and data.'
+---
+
+A supplemental script (`update.sh`) pulls a newer version of the self-hosted Supabase **configuration** on top of your existing deployment. It uses a three-way merge, so your secrets, overrides, and local edits survive, and genuine conflicts are surfaced rather than silently overwritten.
+
+<Admonition type="note" title="No recorded version yet?">
+
+`update.sh` needs to know the release your deployment started from, recorded in a `.supabase-version` file. A recent `setup.sh` writes it, but **older or manually-set-up deployments will not have it**. If yours doesn't, start with [Coming from an old install](#coming-from-an-old-untracked-install) - it is a one-time step.
+
+</Admonition>
+
+## How it works
+
+`update.sh` compares three versions of every vendor file:
+
+- **base** - the release your deployment is currently on, recorded in `.supabase-version` (written by `setup.sh`, then advanced to the new release after each successful update)
+- **new** - the release you are updating to (the latest `self-hosted/v*` tag by default)
+- **user's** - the files in your deployment directory
+
+It applies the changes between base and new to your files. Where you never edited a file, it updates cleanly. Where you edited a file but the release did not touch the same lines, your edit is kept. Only when both changed the same lines do you get a conflict to resolve.
+
+Your secrets and data are never merged. The values in the `.env` file you set are kept (new keys from `.env.example` are appended), and paths listed in `.gitignore` are left untouched.
+
+## Before you start
+
+<Admonition type="danger" title="Back up your database">
+
+`update.sh` backs up your configuration files to `backups/`, but it does not back up Postgres or Storage data. Back those up separately first.
+
+</Admonition>
+
+- Run from your deployment directory (where `docker-compose.yml` and `.env` live).
+- `update.sh` needs `git` and `jq` on the host (both are installed by `setup.sh`).
+- Deployments created with `setup.sh` record their version in `.supabase-version`, and `update.sh` advances it after each successful update. If that file is missing (older or manual installs), refer to [Setting a recorded version](#setting-a-recorded-version).
+
+## How to update
+
+Preview what would change, without affecting anything:
+
+```sh
+sh update.sh --dry-run
+```
+
+Apply the update (targets the latest `self-hosted/v*` release):
+
+```sh
+sh update.sh
+```
+
+Pull the new images and recreate the containers:
+
+```sh
+sh run.sh pull
+sh run.sh recreate
+```
+
+Review the summary `update.sh` prints - especially any conflicts, new `.env` keys, and breaking-change notices.
+
+### What it changes
+
+Updated:
+
+- `docker-compose.yml`, override templates, `volumes/*`, scripts, `.env.example`
+- `volumes/functions/main/index.ts` (vendor bundle)
+
+Excluded:
+
+- Current `.env` configuration
+- Paths in `.gitignore`: data directories, snippets, your edge functions
+
+## Breaking changes
+
+Some releases need a manual step before their files can be applied - for example a Postgres major upgrade. `update.sh` detects these from the release manifest and stops to list them **before** modifying anything, so you can abort with your deployment untouched.
+
+Complete the listed steps (including any `utils/*.sh` migration script mentioned), then re-run `sh update.sh`. Use `--yes` only when you have already done the required work.
+
+## Resolving conflicts
+
+If the summary lists conflicts, `update.sh` has written standard merge markers into those files and exits with status `2`. Open each file, pick the correct content, and remove the `<<<<<<<`, `=======`, and `>>>>>>>` markers:
+
+```yaml
+<<<<<<< yours (docker-compose.yml)
+      image: supabase/studio:your-pinned-tag
+=======
+      image: supabase/studio:new-tag
+>>>>>>> new (self-hosted/v0.7.0)
+```
+
+Then start the stack:
+
+```sh
+sh run.sh pull
+sh run.sh recreate
+```
+
+A conflict means you edited a vendor file and the release changed the same lines. If you enabled auth providers by uncommenting lines in `docker-compose.yml`, this is where you re-apply them when a release reworks those lines.
+
+While conflicts remain, `update.sh` does **not** advance `.supabase-version` - it records the new release only on a clean run. After you resolve the markers, re-run `sh update.sh` to finalize the version stamp.
+
+## Update to a specific release
+
+```sh
+sh update.sh --to self-hosted/v0.7.0
+```
+
+See the [changelog](https://github.com/supabase/supabase/blob/master/docker/CHANGELOG.md) for available releases.
+
+## Setting a recorded version
+
+Without `.supabase-version`, `update.sh` cannot merge safely and runs in **report-only** mode: it lists what is new but writes nothing. Record the version your files came from once, then re-run.
+
+Prefer the **exact commit** your `./docker` files were copied from, if you can find it (for example, from the `git clone` you originally set up from). An exact base gives the cleanest merge - conflicts only where you hand-edited a file that the release also changed:
+
+```sh
+printf 'ref=<commit-sha>\n' > .supabase-version
+```
+
+To find that commit: if you still have the `git clone` of `supabase/supabase` you originally set up from, run `git -C /path/to/your/clone rev-parse HEAD` - it prints the SHA. That is accurate as long as you have not run `git pull` in that clone since you deployed; check the date it points at with `git -C /path/to/your/clone log -1 --format='%H %cd'`. If you no longer have the clone, or are unsure, do not guess - use the closest release tag below, which you can verify against your image tags.
+
+Otherwise use the **closest release tag** - compare the image tags in `docker-compose.yml` / `.env` against [versions.md](https://github.com/supabase/supabase/blob/master/docker/versions.md). This is an approximation, so expect conflicts roughly proportional to how far your files have drifted:
+
+```sh
+printf 'ref=self-hosted/v0.7.0\n' > .supabase-version
+```
+
+Either way, you can supply it for a single run instead of writing the file:
+
+```sh
+sh update.sh --from <commit-sha-or-tag>
+```
+
+{/* supa-mdx-lint-disable-next-line Rule003Spelling */}
+
+## Coming from an old, untracked install
+
+If you have run self-hosted for a long time without `.supabase-version` and your files have drifted from any release, the first catch-up is manual - but you only do it once. After it succeeds the version is recorded, and every future update is a clean, gated merge.
+
+1. **See what is coming.** Run `sh update.sh` once. With no recorded version it stays in report-only mode and lists the new files and `.env` keys without changing anything.
+2. **Record your base** (see [Setting a recorded version](#setting-a-recorded-version)) - an exact commit SHA if you can find it, otherwise the closest tag.
+3. **Preview.** Run `sh update.sh --dry-run` and check the conflict count. If it is overwhelming, try an older or closer base; the better the base matches your real files, the fewer conflicts.
+4. **Back up your database** separately - `update.sh` backs up configuration only.
+5. **Apply**, then resolve any conflict markers (see [Resolving conflicts](#resolving-conflicts)).
+6. **Handle Postgres.** Postgres 17 became the default in v0.6.0. If you are still on Postgres 15, do **not** recreate straight onto 17 - follow [Upgrade to Postgres 17](/docs/guides/self-hosting/postgres-upgrade-17), or pin Postgres 15 with the `docker-compose.pg15.yml` override. Read the [changelog](https://github.com/supabase/supabase/blob/master/docker/CHANGELOG.md) for the breaking changes across the range you are crossing.
+7. Run `sh run.sh pull`, then `sh run.sh recreate`.
+
+<Admonition type="note">
+
+If your deployment has drifted heavily, a clean reinstall at the latest release with `setup.sh` plus a data migration can be less work than resolving a large merge. Consider it if the dry run in step 3 shows an unmanageable number of conflicts.
+
+</Admonition>
+
+## Restore from backup
+
+**Configuration backups** are written to `backups/pre-update-*.tgz` before each update. If something goes wrong, extract or compare against that archive.
+
+## Command-line options
+
+| Flag           | Purpose                                                     |
+| -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `--dry-run`    | Show the plan; write nothing                                |
+| `--to <tag>`   | Update to a specific release                                |
+| `--from <ref>` | Supply the base version when `.supabase-version` is missing |
+| `--yes`        | Skip the breaking-change confirmation prompt                |

